# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
# OnePlus Pad 4: SuperVOOC policy. Perpetua's sc8547_svooc driver comes up
# with charge_types = Fast (sc8547_svooc.c, probe), i.e. it would run a
# SuperVOOC session as soon as a VOOC adapter identifies on a 5 V DCP
# source. In Standard the driver never starts a session (the start gate,
# the retry path and the periodic check all require Fast) and PD charging
# through battmgr is untouched. Until this port's staged validation has
# passed (rung P: the driver picks the session current from the charge
# level — 2 A above 85 %, 3 A from 56 %, up to 6.2 A at 55 % and below —
# so the stages are run at those charge levels with the thermistors
# logged), every boot starts in Standard; the session script opts in
# deliberately (a flag under /run/iceland tells the policy unit to leave
# the opt-in alone). Remove this rule and the unit when the validation
# lands.
#
# Why the rule hands the device to systemd instead of writing the
# attribute (0.2.30 did, and it held only on hand-triggered events): the
# kernel never emits an "add" uevent for this supply — a module reload
# watched with udevadm monitor on 2026-11-03 showed "change" and "remove"
# for it and no "add" — because the power-supply core's add uevent is
# built from get_property calls that fail before the supply is marked
# usable, and a failed uevent is dropped silently. The "change" events do
# arrive, so the device is tagged on add or change and the oneshot policy
# unit runs once udev has finished with it (systemd starts the wanted unit
# when the device is first seen, and again after a remove/re-add).
SUBSYSTEM=="power_supply", KERNEL=="sc8547-svooc", ACTION=="add|change", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="iceland-charging-policy.service"
